                <head>36. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>On December 19, Prime Minister Nehru met
                    with President Eisenhower in Washington
                    and discussed the Kashmir dispute. Immediately following their talk, the
                    President telephoned Secretary Dulles.
                    The following portion of the memorandum of that telephone conversation by
                    Phyllis D. Bernau, Dulles’ secretary,
                    summarizes the discussion relating to Kashmir: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“He [Nehru] said the Pakistan
                            newspapers have one slogan—fight India. The Pres. said if any nation to
                            whom we give arms uses them aggressively we would oppose them—not go to
                            war but exert other pressures. N. said he knows it but they have only
                            one cry. Our newspapers are filled with the five-year plan and they have
                            come to accept the existence of Pakistan and difficulties and they have
                            four problems which he described and they think they can be solved. He
                            asked us to get them to stop the propaganda and threats. The Pres. said
                            we are against it but don’t know how we do it. Nehru said the papers frighten the
                            people. The Pres. said something (I’m not sure of) re maybe their
                            purposes are pure but do the people believe what you say.” (Memorandum
                            of telephone conversation, December 19; Eisenhower Library, Dulles Papers, White House Telephone Conversations)</p>
                    </quote></p>
            </div>
